/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A simple train has one gear on each shaft.
   - For one external pair the driven speed is n2 = −(z1/z2) n1; the minus sign means the direction reverses.
   - nlast/nfirst = ± (product of driver teeth)/(product of driven teeth) The ratio is called the train value; its inverse is the speed ratio i.
   - Example: drivers of 20 and 30 teeth, driven gears of 40 and 60 teeth.
   - A simple epicyclic train has a sun S on the central axis, planets P that mesh with the sun and with an internal ring (annulus) R, and an arm or carrier A that holds the planet pins and turns about the central axis.
   - The train has two degrees of freedom.
   - The planet turns opposite to the sun (external mesh), and the ring turns the same way as the planet (internal mesh).
   - If friction is ignored, the three external torques on the train balance and the power balances: TS + TR + TA = 0 and TS nS + TR nR + TA nA = 0.
   - In a car differential the crown wheel drives the cage (the arm) and bevel planets drive the two half shafts.
*/
