/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Gear life is counted in load cycles on one tooth.
   - The S-N curve plots the alternating stress against the cycles to failure.
   - Miner's rule adds the damage of each load level: D = Σ ni/Ni, where ni is the number of cycles spent at level i and Ni the cycles to failure at that level from the S-N curve.
   - Let a load spectrum give the power Pi (or torque Ti) at each duty point and the fraction pi of the life spent there, at a constant speed.
   - Peq = (Σ pi Pik)1/k Peq is the equivalent power in kW; the fractions pi add to 1; k = 3 for contact fatigue.
   - For bending the exponent is larger, so do not reuse the contact Peq for a bending check.
   - One stage of spur or helical gears suits ratios up to about 5 or 6; beyond that the pinion becomes too small or the gear too large.
   - The housing holds the bearing bores in line, holds the oil and passes heat to the air; ribs stiffen it.
   - Pin (1 − η) = k A (t − t0) Pin is the input power in W, η the overall efficiency (about 0.98 per spur or helical stage), k the heat transfer coefficient in W/(m²K), A the housing area in m², t the oil temperature and t0 the air temperature.
   - A gearbox can fail first on bearing life, oil temperature or a leaking seal, not on its teeth, so each gets its own check; bearings are selected from their load ratings and life in the unit on bearings.
*/
