/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let the spindle have z speeds from nmin to nmax.
   - φ = (nmax/nmin)1/(z − 1) There are z − 1 steps between z speeds; φ has no unit.
   - Geometric steps give the same percentage change everywhere.
   - Standard step ratios come from the preferred number series: 1.06, 1.12, 1.26, 1.41, 1.58 and 2.0 (R40, R20, R10, R20/3, R5 and R10/3 series); 1.26 and 1.41 are the most common in machine tools.
   - The structure formula writes the number of speeds as a product, z = p1 × p2 × …, where p1 is the number of ratios in the first group (nearest the motor), p2 in the second, and so on.
   - A ray diagram (speed chart) draws one vertical line per shaft and one horizontal line per standard speed, spaced equally because the scale is logarithmic in φ.
   - Rules that keep the gears sensible: in any group the reduction should be no more than 1/4 and the step-up no more than 2 (so a group's ratios span at most 8); keep each shaft as fast as possible for as long as possible, so put the big reduc
   - Within one group all pairs share the same two shafts, so they must have the same centre distance: with one module, zdriver + zdriven is the same for every pair of the group.
   - The gearing (kinematic) diagram is the side view of the same design: shafts, the gears on each, and which pairs mesh for each speed.
*/
