/* book Worked example 2: Q: Design the gearbox for these six standard speeds with the structure 6 = 3(1) 2(3). Shaft I runs at 560 rpm. Draw the ray diagram, find the tooth numbers of each pair with one module and check the output speeds.
   1. Group a (shaft I to II), three ratios one step apart: 1, 1/1.41, 1/2, giving 560, 400 and 280 rpm on shaft II. The largest reduction 1/2 is inside the 1/4 limit.
   2. Group b (shaft II to III), two ratios three steps apart: 1 and 1/φ³ = 1/2.8. From 560, 400 and 280 rpm they give 560, 400, 280 and 200, 140, 100 rpm on shaft III: all six speeds, none twice.
   3. Teeth of group a with a sum of 72: 1:1 gives 36/36; 1:1.41 gives 72/2.41 = 29.9, so 30/42; 1:2 gives 24/48.
   4. Teeth of group b with a sum of 76: 1:1 gives 38/38; 1:2.8 gives 76/3.8 = 20, so 20/56. Smallest gear 20 teeth, above the undercut limit.
   5. Actual speeds: 560 × 30/42 = 400; 560 × 24/48 = 280; then × 20/56: 200.0, 142.9 and 100.0 rpm. Only 142.9 differs from the standard 140, by 2.0 %, inside the allowed 4.1 %.
   Answer: Group a: 36/36, 30/42, 24/48; group b: 38/38, 20/56; output speeds 560, 400, 280, 200, 142.9 and 100 rpm. */
